Generative AI tools now pervasively integrate into educational contexts, yet the field lacks empirical understanding of how technical AI configurations translate into distinct pedagogical affordances. Current commercial AI systems treat parameters as hidden technical implementation details, leaving educators unable to deliberately calibrate scaffolding intensity to support creative risk-taking while maintaining linguistic structure. This gap is especially critical in L2 creative writing, where traditional scaffolding approaches face a fundamental tension: excessive structure risks formulaic output and diminished authorship, while insufficient support creates cognitive overload and suppresses creative exploration. This doctoral research addresses this gap by investigating how Large Language Model parameter configuration can function as a pedagogically design lever for adaptive scaffolding in creative writing.To operationalize this investigation, we designed a purpose-built platform (poetry.aitutor.ink) that decouples parameters from prompt design, enabling direct measurement of how technical configurations shape pedagogical outcomes. Through a rigorous experimental design with naturalistic validation, we examined how temperature and top-p parameters—typically treated as opaque technical settings—systematically shape learner interaction patterns, creative agency, and authorship perception in AI-assisted poetry writing. Findings reveal that parameter configuration influence scaffolding mode: exploratory parameters generated creative divergence interactions at 7× the rate of structured parameters (35% vs. 5%), producing 6× higher authorship perception (62.5% vs. 10%) and doubled creative satisfaction. Notably, an analysis uncovered the "helpful but alienating" paradox—learners rated heavily scaffolded interactions as most helpful, yet these same interactions negatively correlated with authorship claims, suggesting educators must distinguish between perceived pedagogical utility and authentic creative agency. These findings establish parameter configuration as a field-worthy research domain, demonstrating that AI scaffolding effectiveness depends not on model capacity alone, but on deliberate configuration choices that remain largely invisible to educators in current commercial systems. This work contributes to AI-in-education by rendering parameter effects explicit and measurable, enabling educators to design responsive AI systems that dynamically adapt scaffolding to learner needs and creative goals.